{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 16, :comments ["<b>And He said.</b> And they walk haughtily, glancing about much, and in the language of the Rabbis: סקרנית." "<b>Making a tinkling [with their feet].</b> Some say it is from the root of speech, like והטף (Ezekiel 21:2), but this is not correct grammatically. Others said that it is derived from the Aramaic translation of הצף (Deuteronomy 11:4), like a person who floats upon the face of the water. And some say it is from the root טף (Esther 3:13), and this is right in my eyes, and the meaning is: walking with the gait of טף." "<b>תעכסנה.</b> They put anklets on their feet like the custom of men who ride. And some say that the anklets produce a sound."], :book :isaiah, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 3, :notes {1 "Root טף in the Esther citation refers to running/swift movement; the comparison is between טפוף and the gait implied by that root."}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Isaiah, by M. Friedlander; Society of Hebrew Literature, London 1877" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH001338443"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:51:56.753202082Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}